In the recent postage discussion, Martin Olah told us how to expropriate
mail delivery from the Pest Orifice, and Perry explained why this was not
an evolutionarily stable strategy if adopted universally[1] because you
have an implicit contract with the P.O., sort of:

>Again, this isn't like being
>forced to pay income tax -- you are buying a service, and there is no
>claim on your part that you are not asking for a service and that they
>are not delivering one to you.

Martin and Perry then anthoropomorphize the State rhetorically:

>> Is it wrong to take from your oppressor?
>
>If you are using his services voluntarily it is. This isn't like
>income taxes.

I want to know, in light of our recent symposium on whether rattlesnakes,
birds, children, morons, fetuses, Jupiter-sized brains, and Capitalized
Extropian Coypu are "people", whether the State is a "person" -- that is,
should we grant the State _any_ of the considerations we grant to other
"people", like not defauding the State or stealing from the State when we
can or not putting the State to a euthanasic end?

Is the "State" most like a vicious armed psychopath who can sometimes be
reasoned with and at other times is just totally berserk?  Or is the
"State" more like a rational (but merciless) person who just has different
opinions than we do about things like property rights?  Or is the "State"
most like a rabid dog, to be dispatched cleanly with a bullet to the head
at the first opportunity, but not to be hated, because it just doesn't know
any better?

If the State is a "person", can we not still justify any kind of nasty
behavior toward it on the grounds that it threw the first punch?  If an
armed thug comes into your tavern, rapes the cocktail waitress and blows up
the jukebox, but stops to buy a pack of smokes on the way out, should you
feel bad about short-changing him?
