From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: ETHICS: Extropian Vices and Virtues

(Sorry to barge in on an ethics debate, something I seem unable to get
interested in usually, but my pal Nietzsche has something to say
here.)

R. David Murray writes:

> Hmm.  That doesn't correspond with my aquired (as opposed to dictionary)
> understanding of 'envy' and 'jealousy'.  The classic 'jealous' is a
> spouse jealous of hir partners's affair: expressed as a desire to
> prevent such activity, regardless of whether or not it affects hir own
> relationship with the spouse.  I've always thought of 'envy' as wanting
> something I didn't have, but not with the intent of depriving someone
> else of it.  So it seems to me you have these two swtiched.  Or is it my
> brain that is switched?

"Envy," as one of the basic "sins" (even if you're not religious, the
recognition of certain facets/truth/rules of getting along is an
important part of culture), is indeed usually associated with the
darker side, of wanting what someone else has. True, there are
harmless uses, as in "I'm envious of Perry for blah blah."

Nietzsche said the most corrosive emotion/attitude is that of
"resentment," which I think he used the French word for
("resentiment"?). By this I think he meant the attitude of extreme
envy/resentment that causes those without BMWs to intensely resent
those who do. The attitude reportedly seen amongst working classes in
Britain, who deeply resent those with means.

In America, I see less of this deep resentment/envy, and more of a
realization that the wealthy have in some sense _earned_ their wealth.
(I mean on a relative scale, compared to the situation elsewhere.) Tom
Wolfe touched on this in "Bonfire of the Vanities" (the book, not the
execrable Bruce Willis vehicle). Things may be pretty bad here, but
this kind of "resentiment" has not yet become the dominant theme.

Rand was influenced early on by Nietzsche, and perhaps this is why
the theme of envy/resentment was so prominent in "The Fountainhead"
and in "Atlas Shrugged." (Yes, I know she later repudiated Nietzsche.
No, I am not saying Nietzsche had all the answers...he's just an
interesting thinker.)

--Tim May



