Date: Wed, 15 Sep 93 13:09:49 PDT
From: thamilto@pcocd2.intel.com (Tony Hamilton - FES ERG~)
Subject: ETHICS: Extropian Vices and Virtues

Tim May says:
> "Envy," as one of the basic "sins" (even if you're not religious, the
> recognition of certain facets/truth/rules of getting along is an
> important part of culture), is indeed usually associated with the
> darker side, of wanting what someone else has. True, there are
> harmless uses, as in "I'm envious of Perry for blah blah."

I'm not entirely sure if you are agreeing or supporting that envy is
somehow unhealthy, but I would disagree with such an assertion in any case.
There is nothing inherently wrong or unhealthy about _any_ instinctive
emotions. They only become unhealthy (or, anti-survivalistic [if that is
a word]) if they are handled or acted upon in certain ways.

Thus, if I find myself envious of someone, I can do a couple of things. I
can remain envious, which gets me nowhere, or I can attempt to figure out
_why_ I feel resentment towards this person for having what I do not. Usually,
I quickly realize that it simply doesn't affect my life one way or another
whether they have something I want, and the resentment goes away. But, the
point is, there is nothing unhealthy happening unless/until the emotion
gets the best of you.

Of course, this is all part of the guilt memes propogated by the Catholic
church (and others) through the years, proposing that one's basic instincts
and emotions can somehow be inherently evil.

